This invention concerns a technique for creating and removing burrs from gear teeth on workpieces (6). The system uses a pair of workpiece spindles (3, 3′), along with a hobbing tool (12) and a deburring apparatus (10). During operation, the workpieces (6) are secured into the spindles (3, 3′) without manual intervention, then gear teeth are cut using the hobbing tool (12), followed by burr removal. The procedure involves these stages:

a. A first workpiece spindle (3) picks up an untreated workpiece (6) at an initial loading station (19) and carries it toward the hobbing tool (12);

b. The milling operation is performed;

c. The workpiece (6) is set back down at the initial loading station (19), and another raw workpiece (6) is collected;

d. The workpiece (6) that has undergone the milling operation is conveyed to the second loading station (19′);

e. Using the second workpiece spindle (3′), the workpiece (6) is retrieved at the second loading station (19′) and delivered to the deburring tool (10);

f. Burrs are removed from the workpiece (6).

In this arrangement, a temporary holding area (14) capable of storing one or more workpieces (6) is positioned between the first loading station (19) and the second loading station (19′). The workpiece (6) travels from this temporary holding area (14) to the loading station (19′). Notably, stages e. and f. occur during the very same time period as stage b.

