# Invention Summary

## Method for Gear-Cutting of Workpieces

The present invention relates to a method for the gear-cutting machining of workpieces using a machine tool having two workpiece spindles (3, 3′), a hobbing tool (12), a deburring device (10), and an intermediate storage (14), whereby the productivity of the machining process is substantially increased through parallelized and overlapping operations.

## Summary of the Invention

The method for gear-cutting machining of workpieces comprises the following steps:

**a)** clamping a first workpiece onto a first workpiece spindle (3) and clamping a second workpiece onto a second workpiece spindle (3′);

**b)** performing a hobbing operation on the first workpiece held by the first workpiece spindle (3) by means of the hobbing tool (12), so as to generate a toothing on the first workpiece;

**c)** transferring the first workpiece, following completion of the hobbing operation, from the first workpiece spindle (3) to the deburring device (10), and deburring the toothing of the first workpiece by means of the deburring device (10);

**d)** depositing the first workpiece, after deburring, into the intermediate storage (14), while simultaneously moving the second workpiece held by the second workpiece spindle (3′) into engagement with the hobbing tool (12) for hobbing;

**e)** performing a hobbing operation on the second workpiece held by the second workpiece spindle (3′) by means of the hobbing tool (12), in a manner temporally overlapping with the deburring and/or the depositing of the first workpiece; and

**f)** removing the first workpiece from the intermediate storage (14) and unloading it, and repeating steps a) through e) for subsequent workpieces such that the two workpiece spindles (3, 3′) are operated in an alternating and mutually overlapping cycle.

## Advantages

By virtue of the alternating operation of the two workpiece spindles (3, 3′) in cooperation with the hobbing tool (12), the deburring device (10), and the intermediate storage (14), the idle times of the hobbing tool (12) are minimized. While the hobbing tool (12) machines the workpiece on one of the workpiece spindles (3, 3′), the previously machined workpiece can be deburred by the deburring device (10) and buffered in the intermediate storage (14). This parallelization of the hobbing, deburring, and handling operations results in a significantly reduced overall cycle time per workpiece and thereby increases the throughput of the machine tool.
